{"parentUuid":"f87344cc-5222-42c4-b71e-98322e924caa","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"cb7ac41e-dae9-4430-be4f-8e4a21dba376","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Fm94AJ2HLToYWDbJoBAADW","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# External Commands\n2\t\n3\t## Overview\n4\t\n5\tThe Entire CLI supports kubectl-style external commands — standalone binaries on `$PATH` that extend the CLI without modifying the main repository. When the user invokes `entire <name>` and `<name>` isn't a built-in subcommand, the CLI looks for an `entire-<name>` binary on `$PATH` and execs it with the remaining arguments. Stdio passes through, exit codes propagate, and the parent CLI does no further processing of the child's output.\n6\t\n7\tThis is **not** the same mechanism as the [external agent protocol](external-agent-protocol.md). External commands have no protocol, no JSON contract, no lifecycle hooks. Use the agent protocol when you need checkpoint integration; use external commands for everything else.\n8\t\n9\t## Resolution\n10\t\n11\tThe CLI does not scan `$PATH` at startup. Resolution is lazy: when `os.Args[1]` doesn't match a built-in subcommand, the CLI calls `exec.LookPath(\"entire-\" + os.Args[1])`. If a binary is found and executable, it runs before Cobra parses arguments.\n12\t\n13\tRules, in order:\n14\t\n15\t1. **Built-ins win.** If the first argument matches a Cobra subcommand (or one of its aliases), the external command is never considered.\n16\t2. **Reserved names are skipped.** Names beginning with `agent-` are reserved for the [agent protocol](external-agent-protocol.md). The resolver refuses to invoke them as external commands.\n17\t3. **Path-traversal candidates are rejected.** Names containing `/` or `\\` never resolve.\n18\t4. **Found-but-not-executable surfaces as a launch error.** If `entire-<name>` exists on `$PATH` but lacks the executable bit, the resolver reports `Failed to run plugin entire-<name>` with exit code 1, rather than falling through to Cobra's \"unknown command\" path.\n19\t\n20\t## Environment\n21\t\n22\tEach external-command invocation receives:\n23\t\n24\t| Variable | Description |\n25\t|---|---|\n26\t| `ENTIRE_CLI_VERSION` | The CLI's version string (e.g. `0.42.0`, `dev`) |\n27\t| `ENTIRE_REPO_ROOT` | Absolute path to the git repository root, when the CLI is invoked inside one. Omitted otherwise. |\n28\t\n29\tPlus the parent's full environment. The working directory is **not** changed — external commands run in the user's current directory, the same as any other shell command.\n30\t\n31\t## Author Contract\n32\t\n33\tExternal commands are arbitrary executables. No SDK, no protocol, no manifest. The contract:\n34\t\n35\t- **Stdio is the parent's terminal.** Stdin, stdout, and stderr are connected directly. The command can prompt interactively, stream output, and behave like any other CLI tool.\n36\t- **Exit codes propagate verbatim.** The parent `entire` exits with the child's exit code.\n37\t- **Signals reach the child.** Terminal signals (Ctrl+C) reach the child directly via the foreground process group. If the parent's context is cancelled (e.g. via `signal.Notify` plumbing), the child receives `SIGINT` with a 5-second grace before the runtime falls back to `SIGKILL`. Commands that need clean shutdown should trap `SIGINT`.\n38\t- **Arguments after the command name pass through verbatim.** `entire pgr --help foo` invokes `entire-pgr` with argv `[\"--help\", \"foo\"]`. Cobra's flag parsing does not run.\n39\t- **Windows.** On Windows, `exec.LookPath` resolves `.exe`, `.bat`, and `.cmd` extensions automatically. The \"found but not executable\" path is Unix-only — Windows treats extension match as the only correctness signal.\n40\t\n41\t## What External Commands Do Not Get\n42\t\n43\t- **No checkpoint integration.** File modifications are not tracked in checkpoints. External commands do not appear in `entire activity`. If a tool needs to participate in the session/checkpoint lifecycle, it must use the [agent protocol](external-agent-protocol.md) instead.\n44\t- **No transcript recording.** External-command stdio is not captured.\n45\t- **No hook installation.** External commands cannot register git hooks or agent hooks via the resolver. They are free to install their own, but `entire` does not coordinate.\n46\t- **No automatic update checks for the command itself.** The CLI runs `versioncheck.CheckAndNotify` for the parent CLI's version, not the child's. Authors should handle their own update notifications.\n47\t\n48\t## Telemetry\n49\t\n50\tExternal-command invocations are tracked only for names on a hardcoded allowlist (`officialPlugins` in `cmd/entire/cli/plugin_official.go`). Third-party command names are **never** sent — even with telemetry opted in. The reasoning matches gh's extension-telemetry posture: arbitrary command names can carry sensitive identifiers (project names, vendor names), and the safest default is silence.\n51\t\n52\tWhen an allowlisted command runs successfully, the CLI emits a `cli_plugin_executed` event with:\n53\t\n54\t- `plugin` — the command name\n55\t- `command` — `entire <name>`\n56\t- `cli_version`, `os`, `arch`, `isEntireEnabled`\n57\t\n58\tArgs and flags are deliberately **not** recorded.\n59\t\n60\tTelemetry fires only when:\n61\t\n62\t1. The command name is in `officialPlugins`.\n63\t2. `entire` settings have `Telemetry: true`.\n64\t3. `ENTIRE_TELEMETRY_OPTOUT` is unset.\n65\t4. The command exited with status 0. Failed/crashing invocations are not tracked, matching Cobra's `PersistentPostRun` semantics for built-in commands.\n66\t\n67\t## Adding an Entire-Shipped Command to the Allowlist\n68\t\n69\tWhen publishing an Entire-owned external command (e.g. `entire-pgr`):\n70\t\n71\t1. Append the command name to `officialPlugins` in `cmd/entire/cli/plugin_official.go`.\n72\t2. Match must be exact and case-sensitive — the binary on disk is `entire-<name>`.\n73\t3. Update or add tests if the command has unusual telemetry shape.\n74\t\n75\tOnce allowlisted, `cli_plugin_executed` events for that command will flow through the existing PostHog pipeline.\n76\t\n77\t## Comparison with the Agent Protocol\n78\t\n79\t| | External Commands | [Agent Protocol](external-agent-protocol.md) |\n80\t|---|---|---|\n81\t| **Binary name pattern** | `entire-<name>` | `entire-agent-<name>` |\n82\t| **Discovery** | Lazy, on first non-built-in arg | Lazy at command entry, gated by `external_agents` setting (setup flows bypass the gate via `DiscoverAndRegisterAlways`) |\n83\t| **Communication** | Process exec; stdio passthrough | Subcommand protocol; JSON over stdin/stdout |\n84\t| **Versioning** | None | `ENTIRE_PROTOCOL_VERSION` envelope |\n85\t| **Lifecycle integration** | None | Full (sessions, checkpoints, hooks, transcripts) |\n86\t| **Telemetry** | Allowlist only | Standard agent telemetry |\n87\t| **Working directory** | User's cwd | Repository root |\n88\t| **Use when** | You want to add a CLI verb | You want an AI agent to participate in checkpointed sessions |\n89\t\n90\t## Implementation\n91\t\n92\tThe resolver lives in `cmd/entire/cli/plugin.go`. The entry point is `MaybeRunPlugin(ctx, rootCmd, args)`, called from `cmd/entire/main.go` before `rootCmd.ExecuteContext`. Returns `(handled bool, exitCode int)` — when `handled` is true, the caller exits with `exitCode`; otherwise it falls through to normal Cobra execution.\n93\t\n94\tKey files:\n95\t\n96\t- `cmd/entire/cli/plugin.go` — entry point, `resolvePlugin`, `runPlugin`\n97\t- `cmd/entire/cli/plugin_official.go` — `officialPlugins` allowlist, `IsOfficialPlugin`\n98\t- `cmd/entire/cli/telemetry/detached.go` — `BuildPluginEventPayload`, `TrackPluginDetached`\n99\t- `cmd/entire/cli/integration_test/external_command_test.go` — end-to-end coverage of the resolution path\n100\t"}]},"uuid":"e788fbf6-eb71-44e0-8eaa-95887cf2c356","timestamp":"2026-05-06T15:51:55.326Z","toolUseResult":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/soph/Work/entire/devenv/cli_experiments_2/docs/architecture/external-commands.md","content":"# External Commands\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Entire CLI supports kubectl-style external commands — standalone binaries on `$PATH` that extend the CLI without modifying the main repository. When the user invokes `entire <name>` and `<name>` isn't a built-in subcommand, the CLI looks for an `entire-<name>` binary on `$PATH` and execs it with the remaining arguments. Stdio passes through, exit codes propagate, and the parent CLI does no further processing of the child's output.\n\nThis is **not** the same mechanism as the [external agent protocol](external-agent-protocol.md). External commands have no protocol, no JSON contract, no lifecycle hooks. Use the agent protocol when you need checkpoint integration; use external commands for everything else.\n\n## Resolution\n\nThe CLI does not scan `$PATH` at startup. Resolution is lazy: when `os.Args[1]` doesn't match a built-in subcommand, the CLI calls `exec.LookPath(\"entire-\" + os.Args[1])`. If a binary is found and executable, it runs before Cobra parses arguments.\n\nRules, in order:\n\n1. **Built-ins win.** If the first argument matches a Cobra subcommand (or one of its aliases), the external command is never considered.\n2. **Reserved names are skipped.** Names beginning with `agent-` are reserved for the [agent protocol](external-agent-protocol.md). The resolver refuses to invoke them as external commands.\n3. **Path-traversal candidates are rejected.** Names containing `/` or `\\` never resolve.\n4. **Found-but-not-executable surfaces as a launch error.** If `entire-<name>` exists on `$PATH` but lacks the executable bit, the resolver reports `Failed to run plugin entire-<name>` with exit code 1, rather than falling through to Cobra's \"unknown command\" path.\n\n## Environment\n\nEach external-command invocation receives:\n\n| Variable | Description |\n|---|---|\n| `ENTIRE_CLI_VERSION` | The CLI's version string (e.g. `0.42.0`, `dev`) |\n| `ENTIRE_REPO_ROOT` | Absolute path to the git repository root, when the CLI is invoked inside one. Omitted otherwise. |\n\nPlus the parent's full environment. The working directory is **not** changed — external commands run in the user's current directory, the same as any other shell command.\n\n## Author Contract\n\nExternal commands are arbitrary executables. No SDK, no protocol, no manifest. The contract:\n\n- **Stdio is the parent's terminal.** Stdin, stdout, and stderr are connected directly. The command can prompt interactively, stream output, and behave like any other CLI tool.\n- **Exit codes propagate verbatim.** The parent `entire` exits with the child's exit code.\n- **Signals reach the child.** Terminal signals (Ctrl+C) reach the child directly via the foreground process group. If the parent's context is cancelled (e.g. via `signal.Notify` plumbing), the child receives `SIGINT` with a 5-second grace before the runtime falls back to `SIGKILL`. Commands that need clean shutdown should trap `SIGINT`.\n- **Arguments after the command name pass through verbatim.** `entire pgr --help foo` invokes `entire-pgr` with argv `[\"--help\", \"foo\"]`. Cobra's flag parsing does not run.\n- **Windows.** On Windows, `exec.LookPath` resolves `.exe`, `.bat`, and `.cmd` extensions automatically. The \"found but not executable\" path is Unix-only — Windows treats extension match as the only correctness signal.\n\n## What External Commands Do Not Get\n\n- **No checkpoint integration.** File modifications are not tracked in checkpoints. External commands do not appear in `entire activity`. If a tool needs to participate in the session/checkpoint lifecycle, it must use the [agent protocol](external-agent-protocol.md) instead.\n- **No transcript recording.** External-command stdio is not captured.\n- **No hook installation.** External commands cannot register git hooks or agent hooks via the resolver. They are free to install their own, but `entire` does not coordinate.\n- **No automatic update checks for the command itself.** The CLI runs `versioncheck.CheckAndNotify` for the parent CLI's version, not the child's. Authors should handle their own update notifications.\n\n## Telemetry\n\nExternal-command invocations are tracked only for names on a hardcoded allowlist (`officialPlugins` in `cmd/entire/cli/plugin_official.go`). Third-party command names are **never** sent — even with telemetry opted in. The reasoning matches gh's extension-telemetry posture: arbitrary command names can carry sensitive identifiers (project names, vendor names), and the safest default is silence.\n\nWhen an allowlisted command runs successfully, the CLI emits a `cli_plugin_executed` event with:\n\n- `plugin` — the command name\n- `command` — `entire <name>`\n- `cli_version`, `os`, `arch`, `isEntireEnabled`\n\nArgs and flags are deliberately **not** recorded.\n\nTelemetry fires only when:\n\n1. The command name is in `officialPlugins`.\n2. `entire` settings have `Telemetry: true`.\n3. `ENTIRE_TELEMETRY_OPTOUT` is unset.\n4. The command exited with status 0. Failed/crashing invocations are not tracked, matching Cobra's `PersistentPostRun` semantics for built-in commands.\n\n## Adding an Entire-Shipped Command to the Allowlist\n\nWhen publishing an Entire-owned external command (e.g. `entire-pgr`):\n\n1. Append the command name to `officialPlugins` in `cmd/entire/cli/plugin_official.go`.\n2. Match must be exact and case-sensitive — the binary on disk is `entire-<name>`.\n3. Update or add tests if the command has unusual telemetry shape.\n\nOnce allowlisted, `cli_plugin_executed` events for that command will flow through the existing PostHog pipeline.\n\n## Comparison with the Agent Protocol\n\n| | External Commands | [Agent Protocol](external-agent-protocol.md) |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Binary name pattern** | `entire-<name>` | `entire-agent-<name>` |\n| **Discovery** | Lazy, on first non-built-in arg | Lazy at command entry, gated by `external_agents` setting (setup flows bypass the gate via `DiscoverAndRegisterAlways`) |\n| **Communication** | Process exec; stdio passthrough | Subcommand protocol; JSON over stdin/stdout |\n| **Versioning** | None | `ENTIRE_PROTOCOL_VERSION` envelope |\n| **Lifecycle integration** | None | Full (sessions, checkpoints, hooks, transcripts) |\n| **Telemetry** | Allowlist only | Standard agent telemetry |\n| **Working directory** | User's cwd | Repository root |\n| **Use when** | You want to add a CLI verb | You want an AI agent to participate in checkpointed sessions |\n\n## Implementation\n\nThe resolver lives in `cmd/entire/cli/plugin.go`. The entry point is `MaybeRunPlugin(ctx, rootCmd, args)`, called from `cmd/entire/main.go` before `rootCmd.ExecuteContext`. Returns `(handled bool, exitCode int)` — when `handled` is true, the caller exits with `exitCode`; otherwise it falls through to normal Cobra execution.\n\nKey files:\n\n- `cmd/entire/cli/plugin.go` — entry point, `resolvePlugin`, `runPlugin`\n- `cmd/entire/cli/plugin_official.go` — `officialPlugins` allowlist, `IsOfficialPlugin`\n- `cmd/entire/cli/telemetry/detached.go` — `BuildPluginEventPayload`, `TrackPluginDetached`\n- `cmd/entire/cli/integration_test/external_command_test.go` — end-to-end coverage of the resolution path\n","numLines":100,"startLine":1,"totalLines":100}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"f87344cc-5222-42c4-b71e-98322e924caa","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/soph/Work/entire/devenv/cli_experiments_2","sessionId":"d3f01bb0-fb75-46a6-ae70-8aab0a703055","version":"2.1.129","gitBranch":"soph/external-command-support"}
